21
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,600
 based off of the ThreatLocker ACL.

22
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,740
 Windows filtering platform, similar situation,

23
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:09,500
 but for connect bind requests, right?

24
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,260
 You can say, okay, hey, is this process allowed to talk

25
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:14,180
 to port 443 over there on the internet?

26
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:15,100
 Yes or no?

27
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:16,240
 Approve or deny.

28
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:19,060
 Lastly, you've got EX version kernel notification routines.

29
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:21,960
 The EX version is important because it allows you to approve

30
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:25,160
 or deny versus just getting a notification from the kernel.

31
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:29,600
 And this is specific for hooking various native API calls

32
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:32,160
 like nt create user process, which is what you would use

